      "content": "Certainly, inspecting the ductwork for leaks or disconnections can be done without tearing out all the drywall. Here are some signs and methods you can use to check the ductwork:\n\n1. **Visual Inspection**: Look for any visible signs of damage or disconnection on the accessible parts of the ductwork. This includes checking for cracks, holes, or loose connections.\n\n2. **Listen for Airflow**: With the AC unit turned on, listen for the sound of air moving through the ducts. If you hear a strong airflow in one room but not in another, it could indicate a leak or disconnection.\n\n3. **Use a Duct Blaster**: A duct blaster is a tool that can help you detect leaks in the ductwork. It blows air through the ducts, and you can listen for escaping air or feel for changes in air pressure.\n\n4. **Smoke Test**: For a more thorough inspection, you can use a smoke pencil or smoke bomb to fill the ducts with smoke. Then, turn on the AC unit and look for any smoke escaping from the ducts, which would indicate a leak.\n\n5. **Thermal Imaging Camera**: A thermal imaging camera can detect temperature differences in the ductwork, helping",
